n{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e39062c0-8f9b-52cc-adaa-1df745d5f896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436685 #edb/ref 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot columns. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and the $4$th columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1+1+2=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436686 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436686 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "034d01d2-7fd8-5b65-910e-ac88f27f255e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436686 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186321072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436687 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436687 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4760dd1d-3298-58a3-b976-41ff6b4b2fae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436687 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436688 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5674bd5f-5ced-5ae8-83dc-206575be79e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436688 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186436685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186436688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5703fb70-6ee8-588c-9bee-0fb013bf758c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436689 #edb/re